Codex model disclosure belongs to turnbridge**, not cledger.\n\n   Cledger already records `producer.model` when the source states it. Turnbridge’s Codex adapter is the component that cannot preserve it and therefore owes the disclosure.\n\n## Branch and document cleanup first\n\nI would not merge `design/policy-split` wholesale. It contains the desired docs, but its source changes predate the annals rename and would revert `stream` back to `conversation`; its lockfile also reflects the older cledger package state.\n\nInstead:\n\n1. Start from `annals-cledger`.\n2. Bring over these files from `design/policy-split`:\n\n   - `docs/LINEAGE_AND_COMPOUNDING.md`\n   - `docs/POLICY_SPLIT.md`\n   - `docs/TODO_HANDOFF.md`\n\n3. Rewrite section A of `TODO_HANDOFF.md` to reflect the final producer-owned-reader decision and point to annals’ relevant extension contract.\n4. Leave cledger/annals-owned design documents in their owning repositories.\n5. Add the requested relocation footer to cross-project handoff/design docs, for example:\n\n   > If this document describes work owned by another project, move the applicable material into that project before implementing it; do not maintain a second authoritative copy here.\n\n   I would not put that on ordinary product docs or format specs—only documents that intentionally discuss cross-project work.\n\n## Implementation sequence\n\nI’d use small, independently reviewable PRs:\n\n### PR 0 — Base and documentation\n\n- Land `annals-cledger` after cledger’s migration completes.\n- Import the three turnbridge design docs without the stale source changes.\n- Rewrite `TODO_HANDOFF.md` section A.\n- Update terminology from cledger-specific envelope concepts where annals is now authoritative.\n- Run the real-ledger smoke test after migration, not just fixtures.\n\nAcceptance check: the picker sees the expected migrated conversations and all tests pass.\n\n### PR 1 — Native annals filters\n\n- Replace the fetch-everything workaround with:\n\n  ```ts\n  readEvents(repo, {\n    ...,\n    kind: [\"conversation_turn\", \"reasoning\"],\n  })\n  ```\n\n- Add a test proving unrelated extension kinds are excluded.\n- Add or retain coverage for both included kinds.\n\n### PR 2 — Provenance correctness\n\n- Read model identity from `producer.model`, not `actor.id`.\n- Read provider from `producer.provider`.\n- Preserve “unset means the source did not state it”; do not infer a provider from model-name syntax.\n- Add compatibility behavior only if migrated events legitimately lack those fields.\n\n### PR 3 — Disclosure cleanup\n\n- Add the missing Codex model-id-drop disclosure.\n- Move human-only information into `plan.notes`:\n\n  - model substitution details;\n  - ancestor-unavailable warnings;\n  - transcript size reporting.\n\n- Keep only information useful to the receiving model in the import notice.\n- Test that notes reach the terminal but do not enter imported model context.\n\nThis closes item 5.\n\n### PR 4 — OpenCode reasoning marker reduction\n\n- Render OpenCode plaintext reasoning as a native reasoning part.\n- Stop inserting `[visible thinking]` on that path.\n- First test whether OpenCode accepts the fabricated reasoning shape reliably.\n- If rejection can leave a partial session, either make fabrication atomic or defer the native form and record that explicitly in `TODO_HANDOFF.md`.\n\nThat was my 3(b) concern: not that the design is dubious, but that a failed multi-file OpenCode import may leave a half-created conversation. A focused PR makes that easy to probe and contain.\n\n### PR 5 — Precise continuation provenance\n\nBefore implementing the transitive walk:\n\n- extend continuation content with ordered source-event identifiers;\n- version the turnbridge continuation payload;\n- record enough target correspondence to distinguish skipped or combined events;\n- update `readLineage` so consumers do not parse payloads themselves.\n\nFor your one historical continuation, either write a one-time backfill or document it as legacy/coarse.\n\n### PR 6 — Multi-hop reconstruction\n\n- Walk `parentOf` transitively.\n- Partition the imported history by per-turn origin.\n- Render each turn from the conversation where it originated.\n- Apply the current target adapter to that original evidence—“as it would have been,” undoing only turnbridge-authored transport degradation.\n- Drop ancestral turnbridge notices.\n- Emit one notice describing the full origin chain.\n- If an original is unavailable, use the captured copy and disclose that fallback.\n- Detect cycles and malformed lineage defensively.\n\n### Deferred investigation — Claude notice representation\n\nMy 3(d) point was simply that the docs disagree about whether Claude `system` or `attachment` records actually enter resumed model context. That is empirical research, not required for the other fixes. Put it in the TODO list until someone can run a controlled resume probe. The existing counterfeit user turn can remain temporarily because it is known to reach context.\n\n## Prompt for the cledger agent\n\nYou can append this to its current assignment:\n\n```text\nDocumentation ownership cleanup:\n\nAfter completing the annals migration, inspect any design or handoff documents\nin conversation-ledger and annals that discuss work owned by turnbridge.\n\n- Move turnbridge-owned implementation guidance into the turnbridge repository,\n  or identify the exact source document and section for the turnbridge agent to\n  move. Do not keep two authoritative copies.\n- Keep cledger- and annals-owned contracts in their owning repositories.\n- For cross-project handoff/design documents that may contain work owned\n  elsewhere, append this instruction:\n\n  \"If this document describes work owned by another project, move the\n  applicable material into that project before implementing it; do not\n  maintain a second authoritative copy here.\"\n\n- Do not append that instruction indiscriminately to ordinary API reference,\n  product, or format-specification documents.\n- Report every document moved, retained, or annotated and why.\n```\n\nSo the immediate gate is straightforward: let the cledger agent finish and validate the migration, then I start from `annals-cledger` with PR 0. After that, each behavioral change can land independently without dragging the multi-hop work into every review.","started_at":1790205552,"time_to_first_token_ms":3423,"turn_id":"01a0d090-e688-75a1-9d69-374fc3927ffd"},"context":{"branch":"continuation-source-events","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"3c28c765c2c1653fc99494c6ea0ad2081f7b48f8","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"id":"ev2-819c70bc0d8bed1c5e24bc9ab2497ad1b140f156122610fa447837d28870e2ef","kind":"activity","meta":{"actor":{"type":"agent"},"model":"gpt-5.6-sol","provider":"openai","session_id":"375a062e-4769-4548-82b0-af1a929dc456","source":"codex","source_version":"0.156.1"},"occurred_at":"2026-09-23T23:20:56.580Z","producer":{"tool":"cledger","version":"0.26.0"},"raw":{"data":{"payload":{"completed_at":1790205656,"duration_ms":104306,"last_agent_message":"Yes—given that this is unpublished, only you have legacy data, and cledger is performing a backed-up one-time migration, I withdraw the backwards-compatibility objection. A permanent annals compatibility layer would be unnecessary baggage.\n\nYour conclusions are sound, with a few implementation details.\n\n## Revised calls\n\n1. **Legacy envelopes:** migration is the right answer.\n\n   The migration should verify all repositories before considering itself complete:\n\n   - old and new event counts reconcile;\n   - every old conversation/stream remains discoverable;\n   - old notes remain under a separate backup ref;\n   - new writes use `annals/v1`, `stream`, and `ev2-…`;\n   - rerunning the migration is harmless.\n\n   Keeping the originals in a backup notes ref is better than deleting them.\n\n2. **Extension discovery:** comfortable deferring it.\n