 OpenCode reasoning marker reduction\n\n- Render OpenCode plaintext reasoning as a native reasoning part.\n- Stop inserting `[visible thinking]` on that path.\n- First test whether OpenCode accepts the fabricated reasoning shape reliably.\n- If rejection can leave a partial session, either make fabrication atomic or defer the native form and record that explicitly in `TODO_HANDOFF.md`.\n\nThat was my 3(b) concern: not that the design is dubious, but that a failed multi-file OpenCode import may leave a half-created conversation. A focused PR makes that easy to probe and contain.\n\n### PR 5 — Precise continuation provenance\n\nBefore implementing the transitive walk:\n\n- extend continuation content with ordered source-event identifiers;\n- version the turnbridge continuation payload;\n- record enough target correspondence to distinguish skipped or combined events;\n- update `readLineage` so consumers do not parse payloads themselves.\n\nFor your one historical continuation, either write a one-time backfill or document it as legacy/coarse.\n\n### PR 6 — Multi-hop reconstruction\n\n- Walk `parentOf` transitively.\n- Partition the imported history by per-turn origin.\n- Render each turn from the conversation where it originated.\n- Apply the current target adapter to that original evidence—“as it would have been,” undoing only turnbridge-authored transport degradation.\n- Drop ancestral turnbridge notices.\n- Emit one notice describing the full origin chain.\n- If an original is unavailable, use the captured copy and disclose that fallback.\n- Detect cycles and malformed lineage defensively.\n\n### Deferred investigation — Claude notice representation\n\nMy 3(d) point was simply that the docs disagree about whether Claude `system` or `attachment` records actually enter resumed model context. That is empirical research, not required for the other fixes. Put it in the TODO list until someone can run a controlled resume probe. The existing counterfeit user turn can remain temporarily because it is known to reach context.\n\n## Prompt for the cledger agent\n\nYou can append this to its current assignment:\n\n```text\nDocumentation ownership cleanup:\n\nAfter completing the annals migration, inspect any design or handoff documents\nin conversation-ledger and annals that discuss work owned by turnbridge.\n\n- Move turnbridge-owned implementation guidance into the turnbridge repository,\n  or identify the exact source document and section for the turnbridge agent to\n  move. Do not keep two authoritative copies.\n- Keep cledger- and annals-owned contracts in their owning repositories.\n- For cross-project handoff/design documents that may contain work owned\n  elsewhere, append this instruction:\n\n  \"If this document describes work owned by another project, move the\n  applicable material into that project before implementing it; do not\n  maintain a second authoritative copy here.\"\n\n- Do not append that instruction indiscriminately to ordinary API reference,\n  product, or format-specification documents.\n- Report every document moved, retained, or annotated and why.\n```\n\nSo the immediate gate is straightforward: let the cledger agent finish and validate the migration, then I start from `annals-cledger` with PR 0. After that, each behavioral change can land independently without dragging the multi-hop work into every review.","started_at":1790205552,"time_to_first_token_ms":3423,"turn_id":"01a0d090-e688-75a1-9d69-374fc3927ffd","type":"task_complete"},"timestamp":"2026-09-23T23:20:56.580Z","type":"event_msg"},"format":"codex-rollout-jsonl/2"},"recorded_at":"2026-09-24T01:59:32.386Z","schema":"annals/v1","stream":{"id":"codex:375a062e-4769-4548-82b0-af1a929dc456","seq":110}}
